WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.636
The fraud team counts card authorizations for merchant m-7.

2
00:00:06.636 --> 00:00:11.439
Each one arrives as an event, sent by the payment service to a consumer.

3
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:16.482
Each event carries the time the card was used, stamped by the payment service.

4
00:00:16.482 --> 00:00:17.850
That's its event time.

5
00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:21.083
The moment the consumer reads it is processing time.

6
00:00:21.267 --> 00:00:23.419
Usually they're milliseconds apart.

7
00:00:23.419 --> 00:00:29.877
But stop the consumer for a minute to redeploy it, and that minute's events pile up, then arrive at once.

8
00:00:30.333 --> 00:00:37.421
Counted by processing time, that's a minute of zeros, then a spike of 2,000 a second that never happened.

9
00:00:37.421 --> 00:00:40.459
Counted by event time, it's 100 every second.

10
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:42.160
So count by event time.

11
00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:47.938
Each one-minute window collects the events that happened inside it, whenever they arrive.

12
00:00:47.938 --> 00:00:49.561
Here, one event a second.

13
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.132
But when is a window finished?

14
00:00:53.132 --> 00:00:57.875
An event from 59 seconds can still show up after one from 65.

15
00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.689
A watermark answers that.

16
00:00:59.689 --> 00:01:05.473
It's the latest event time seen so far, and it claims that nothing earlier is still coming.

17
00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:10.436
That's a guess, so each window also waits a grace period, here 10 seconds.

18
00:01:10.436 --> 00:01:13.787
It closes when the watermark passes its end plus 10.

19
00:01:13.967 --> 00:01:15.895
The watermark reaches 65.

20
00:01:15.895 --> 00:01:19.907
Now a straggler arrives that happened at 59 seconds.

21
00:01:19.907 --> 00:01:23.224
Its window is still open, so it counts: 61.

22
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.084
At 70, the window's end plus its grace, it closes.

23
00:01:27.084 --> 00:01:31.136
Its count, 61, goes out once, and its state is dropped.

24
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:36.213
The next window closes the same way, at 130, with 60 events.

25
00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:39.451
At 150, an event from 100 seconds arrives.

26
00:01:39.451 --> 00:01:45.343
Its window is gone, so the event is dropped as late, and the late counter goes up.

27
00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:50.605
A 60 second grace would have kept it, but every window would then report a minute later.

28
00:01:50.833 --> 00:01:55.555
Choosing the grace period is choosing between late results and lost events.
